      content: `**What is agreed.** Judgment follows death (Hebrews 9:27), and every historic Christian position except universalism holds that its outcome is final and irreversible. The debate among those who accept the authority of Scripture is narrower: whether the lost suffer consciously without end, or are punished and then destroyed forever[^26].

**Eternal conscious punishment: the traditional view.** Held by Augustine, Aquinas, the Reformers and the major Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant confessions, it remains the majority view[^27]. Its main texts:

*   **Matthew 25:46.** "These will go away into eternal punishment, but the righteous into eternal life." The same adjective, *aiōnios*, describes both destinies in the same sentence; if the life is unending, the natural reading is that the punishment is too. This parallel is the view's central argument[^28].
*   **Revelation 14:10-11.** Those who worship the beast are "tormented with fire and sulfur", and "the smoke of their torment goes up forever and ever, and they have no rest, day or night"[^29].
*   **Revelation 20:10 and Matthew 25:41.** The devil is "tormented day and night forever and ever" in the lake of fire. Jesus sends the lost into "the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels", the same fire, and Revelation 20:15 throws into that lake everyone whose name is not in the book of life. Robert Peterson regards this as the strongest traditional text, because the devil is a personal being and his punishment is explicitly endless[^30].
*   **Mark 9:48 and Daniel 12:2.** Hell is where "their worm does not die and the fire is not quenched", and Daniel speaks of some rising "to shame and everlasting contempt"[^31][^32].

**Annihilationism: conditional immortality.** A significant evangelical minority, including John Stott (tentatively, in *Evangelical Essentials*, 1988), Edward Fudge (*The Fire That Consumes*, 1982) and Clark Pinnock, holds that the lost are raised, judged, punished in proportion to their sins, and then destroyed forever[^33][^34][^35]. Its arguments:

*   **The Bible's commonest language is destruction.** God can "destroy both soul and body in hell" (Matthew 10:28); the lost "perish" (John 3:16); "the wages of sin is death" (Romans 6:23); their "end is destruction" (Philippians 3:19); they "suffer the punishment of eternal destruction" (2 Thessalonians 1:9); and the lake of fire is "the second death" (Revelation 20:14)[^36][^37][^38].
*   **"Eternal" can describe a result.** Hebrews 9:12 speaks of "eternal redemption", accomplished once with permanent effect. On this reading "eternal punishment" and "eternal destruction" are a penalty whose result, not whose process, lasts forever[^39][^40].
*   **Sodom's "eternal fire".** Jude 7 says Sodom and Gomorrah "serve as an example by undergoing a punishment of eternal fire". That fire burned out; what was eternal was the destruction[^41].
*   **The traditional texts reread.** Mark 9:48 quotes Isaiah 66:24, where the worm and the fire consume "the dead bodies of the men who have rebelled", corpses rather than living people[^42]. The smoke rising "forever and ever" in Revelation 14:11 echoes Isaiah 34:10, where the smoke of Edom's ruin "shall go up forever" as a lasting sign that the destruction is complete[^43]. Revelation 20:10 concerns the devil and two symbolic figures, the beast and the false prophet, rather than human beings. Malachi 4:3 says the wicked "will be ashes under the soles of your feet"[^44].
*   **Immortality is a gift.** Scripture says God "alone has immortality" (1 Timothy 6:16) and offers immortality to believers with eternal life; on this view the lost have no endless existence unless God grants it[^45].

**The traditional replies.** Traditionalists answer that human beings bear God's image and were made to exist permanently[^46], and that 1 Timothy 6:16 speaks of God's underived immortality, not of whether creatures can be kept in being forever[^47]. They note that "no rest, day or night" in Revelation 14:11 describes continuing experience rather than a memorial, and that "destroy" (*apollymi*) can mean ruin rather than ceasing to exist, as when wineskins burst and "are destroyed" (Matthew 9:17)[^48]. Peterson adds that the beast "goes to destruction" in Revelation 17:8 yet is "tormented day and night forever and ever" in Revelation 20:10, so destruction and endless torment are not opposites in Revelation's own usage.

**Where the question stands.** The finality of judgment is taught plainly and denied only by universalism. Eternal conscious punishment is the historic teaching of the church and rests on the most natural reading of Matthew 25:46 and Revelation 14:11 and 20:10. Annihilationism remains a minority position; a commission of the Evangelical Alliance in the UK concluded in 2000 that it is a "significant minority evangelical view" compatible with the Alliance's Basis of Faith[^49]. Both views agree that hell is real, final and just, that it is the loss of God himself, the source of every good, and that it is to be avoided at all costs[^50].`
