      id: "degrees-of-punishment",
      title: "Are There Degrees or Levels of Punishment in Hell?",
      tags: ["degrees", "judgment", "proportion", "dante"],
      content: `**Scripture teaches degrees of punishment.** Several passages say that the lost will not all be judged alike.

*   **Knowledge.** The servant who knew his master's will and did not do it "will receive a severe beating", but the one who did not know "will receive a light beating. Everyone to whom much was given, of him much will be required" (Luke 12:47-48)[^98].
*   **Opportunity.** Jesus told the Galilean towns that had seen his miracles that "it will be more bearable on the day of judgment for Tyre and Sidon than for you" and for Sodom than for Capernaum (Matthew 11:22-24; compare Matthew 10:15)[^99][^100].
*   **Hypocrisy and abuse of power.** The scribes who "devour widows' houses" will "receive the greater condemnation" (Mark 12:40). Jesus told Pilate that the one who handed him over "has the greater sin" (John 19:11)[^101][^102].
*   **Deeds.** God "will render to each one according to his works" (Romans 2:6), and Hebrews 10:29 asks "how much worse punishment" is deserved by one who has trampled underfoot the Son of God[^103][^104].

**The church has long taught the same.** Augustine wrote that "the mildest punishment of all" falls on those with no actual sin added to original sin, and that for the rest "the punishment of each will be the more tolerable in the next world, according as his iniquity has been less in this world" (*Enchiridion* 93)[^105]. The Council of Florence (1439) defined that the lost go down to hell "to be punished, but with unequal pains"[^106]. Protestant writers teach the same, tying the severity of judgment to a person's deeds and to the light they rejected[^107][^108][^109].

**Will it fit the crime?** On this teaching, yes. The duration is the same for all the lost on the traditional view, but the severity is not: no one suffers more than their guilt deserves, and each is judged by what they knew and did. Abraham's question, "Shall not the Judge of all the earth do what is just?" (Genesis 18:25), is the assumption on which the whole doctrine rests[^110]. Annihilationists apply the same texts to the suffering that precedes final destruction.

**Levels and circles.** The nine descending circles of hell, each holding a particular kind of sinner, come from Dante's *Inferno* (early 14th century), a poem rather than a statement of doctrine. Scripture teaches degrees of punishment but gives no map, number of levels or arrangement of sins[^111].`
